parts.body:
Under 11 NYCRR 30.3, a producer selling an insurance contract must make a [[producer-compensation-disclosure]] to the purchaser, **orally or in a prominent writing, at or before application**:

1. the producer's **role** in the sale;
2. **whether** the producer will be compensated by the insurer or another third party based on the sale;
3. that compensation **may vary** (by contract and insurer selected, volume of business, or profitability); and
4. that the purchaser **may request** more information about the compensation.

If the purchaser asks **before issuance**, the producer must give a prominent written disclosure of the nature, amount and source of compensation, alternative quotes, material ownership interests, and whether the producer is barred from altering compensation — at or before issuance (or within **five business days** if time is of the essence). If asked **within 30 days after issuance**, the written disclosure is due within **five business days**. An oral basic disclosure must be followed in writing no later than issuance, and producers may not contradict or mislead about their role or pay.
